WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.570
The most durable metaphor in enzymology escaped almost by accident.

2
00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:13.370
Closing an 1894 lecture, Fischer reached for an image to explain why an enzyme would

3
00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:16.510
tear one sugar apart and ignore its near-twin.

4
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.210
enzyme and substrate had to fit "like lock and key."

5
00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:22.770
He distrusted it the moment he said it.

6
00:00:23.433 --> 00:00:28.913
By 1898 he was publicly walking it back, insisting the specificity lay in "the

7
00:00:28.913 --> 00:00:33.713
asymmetric structure of the enzyme molecule" and that he had used the picture only

8
00:00:33.713 --> 00:00:34.933
to make the thought clearer.

9
00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:40.557
Then the warning this chapter proves him right to give: the idea "can only be

10
00:00:40.557 --> 00:00:45.577
thoroughly tested when we are able to isolate the enzymes in a pure state and thus

11
00:00:45.577 --> 00:00:47.097
investigate their configuration."

12
00:00:48.037 --> 00:00:49.457
It took most of a century.

13
00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:55.190
When crystallographers finally held a pure enzyme and looked, the brass lock turned

14
00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.990
out to flex closing around its substrate, and gripping the fleeting transition state

15
00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:04.590
tighter still. That correction is what Koshland named induced fit.

16
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:09.483
Fischer reached the lock and key idea the way he read structure off behavior

17
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:14.843
everywhere else: he built sugars from glycerol to prove life held no chemistry

18
00:01:14.843 --> 00:01:19.803
beyond the ordinary, declaring that science had stripped the life force of "this

19
00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:21.043
last hiding place.

20
00:01:21.603 --> 00:01:26.983
The phenylhydrazine that fingerprinted those sugars also, slowly, poisoned him; in

21
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:31.383
1919, dying of an inoperable cancer, he took his own life.
